  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"246\">The <strong data-start=\"119\" data-end=\"139\">Chromium &ndash; Serum</strong> test measures the concentration of chromium in the blood serum. Chromium is a trace element essential for:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"248\" data-end=\"346\">\n<li data-start=\"248\" data-end=\"279\">\n<p data-start=\"250\" data-end=\"279\"><strong data-start=\"250\" data-end=\"279\">Normal glucose metabolism</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"313\">\n<p data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"313\"><strong data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"313\">Potentiating insulin action</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"314\" data-end=\"346\">\n<p data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"346\"><strong data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"346\">Fat and protein metabolism</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"348\" data-end=\"382\">This test is typically ordered to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"686\">\n<li data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"516\">\n<p data-start=\"386\" data-end=\"516\">Assess <strong data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"415\">nutritional status</strong>, especially in patients on <strong data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"479\">total parenteral nutrition (TPN)</strong> or those with suspected deficiency</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"517\" data-end=\"555\">\n<p data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"555\">Monitor <strong data-start=\"527\" data-end=\"555\">chromium supplementation</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"556\" data-end=\"686\">\n<p data-start=\"558\" data-end=\"686\">Evaluate potential <strong data-start=\"577\" data-end=\"598\">chromium toxicity</strong> from <strong data-start=\"604\" data-end=\"663\">environmental, occupational, or medical device exposure</strong> (e.g., metal implants)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cause / Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Insight</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Excessive supplementation</strong></td>\n<td>High doses of chromium supplements can elevate serum levels and potentially be toxic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metal-on-metal joint implants</strong></td>\n<td>Corrosion or wear may release chromium into the bloodstream</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Industrial/occupational exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Inhalation or dermal contact with chromium compounds (especially hexavalent Cr⁶⁺)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Renal impairment</strong></td>\n<td>Reduced excretion may lead to accumulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
